\description{
Run the BOIN design with backfilling (BF-BOIN) of Zhao et al. (2024) over
  many simulated trials. Dose escalation proceeds in fully staggered cohorts
  with the BOIN decision table; patients who arrive while the escalation waits
  for the DLT assessment of its current cohort are backfilled to a lower dose
  that is safe and has shown a response. Return the patient, DLT, backfill and
  response counts at every dose together with the duration of each trial.
}
\details{
\strong{Dose escalation.} Cohorts are fully staggered: the next cohort
  starts only when every patient of the current one has completed the DLT
  assessment. The decision then follows the BOIN decision table applied to
  the patients at the current dose who have completed the assessment,
  backfilled ones included; pending backfill patients are left out, as in
  Zhao et al. (2024). Dose elimination is checked at the same moments, with
  the same completed patients, at every dose.

  \strong{Backfilling.} A patient who arrives while the escalation waits is
  backfilled to the highest dose b (or the lowest, with
  \code{backfill_dose = "lowest"}) that is below the current dose, has not
  been eliminated, has fewer than \code{n_cap} patients, and has a response
  observed at b or at a lower dose. A dose is closed for backfilling when its
  own DLT rate exceeds the de-escalation boundary and so does the rate pooled
  with the next higher dose; the doses above it close with it. The rules are
  evaluated whenever a patient arrives, so a closed dose reopens when new data
  no longer meet the closing condition.

  \strong{Conflicts.} When the data of a backfilled dose b below the current
  dose call for a more conservative decision than those of the current dose
  (Table 2 of Zhao et al., 2024), the decision is taken instead from the data
  pooled from dose b* to the current dose, where b* is the highest
  conflicting dose by default. A de-escalation then goes to the highest dose
  k at or above b* whose data pooled from b* stay at or below the
  de-escalation boundary, or to b* - 1 if there is none, and to the lowest
  dose if b* is the lowest.

  \strong{End of the trial.} The trial stops when the escalation has treated
  \code{n_cohort} cohorts, when \code{n_earlystop} is reached with a decision
  to stay, or when the lowest dose is eliminated. Backfilling stops with the
  escalation. Every enrolled patient is followed to the end of the
  assessment, and the data of all patients, backfilled ones included, are
  those from which the MTD is selected.

  \strong{Arrivals and outcomes.} Patients arrive one at a time. The DLT of a
  patient and its time follow \code{p_true} and \code{dlt_time} as in
  \code{\link{tite_boin_simulate}}; the response and its time follow
  \code{p_resp}, \code{resp_window} and \code{resp_late_fraction} in the same
  way, linked to the DLT by \code{resp_cor}. Neither article states how
  responses are timed or correlated with DLTs. The Weibull time to response
  with half of the responses in the second half of the window and the
  Gaussian copula follow Takeda et al. (2025), who set the correlation to
  0.2; the default here is independence.

  \strong{Random numbers.} The DLT variates of the escalation patients are
  drawn from R's stream exactly as in \code{\link{boin_simulate}}; arrivals
  and the variates of backfill patients and of responses come from two
  generators of their own seeded with \code{seed}. As a result, when no dose
  is ever opened for backfilling, for example with every \code{p_resp} equal
  to 0, the trials are identical to those of \code{\link{boin_simulate}} with
  the same seed.

  \strong{Agreement with the article.} With \code{stay_on_1_of_3 = TRUE},
  \code{n_earlystop = 9}, \code{accrual = "uniform"} and
  \code{no_slot = "leave"}, the operating characteristics of Table 4 of Zhao
  et al. (2024) are reproduced within simulation error, durations included.
  The article states a Poisson process, but its durations, for BOIN as well
  as for BF-BOIN, agree with uniform times between arrivals and patients
  turned away while the escalation waits. The trial example of Figure 1 of
  the article is replayed patient by patient in the tests of the package.
}
